cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "LUNA: A Differentiable Astrophysical Hydrodynamics Code"
abstract: >-
  LUNA is a differentiable astrophysical hydrodynamics code written in JAX, with hand-written
  Pallas kernels as a fast path on GPUs. It solves the Euler equations in one, two and three
  dimensions with an unsplit, second-order MUSCL-Hancock scheme and HLLC or HLL Riemann solvers,
  and runs on CPUs and on many NVIDIA or AMD GPUs, with the domain split over devices.
  Self-gravity is solved with periodic or isolated boundaries and couples to the gas conserving
  energy; comoving cosmology evolves gas together with dark-matter particles; radiative cooling,
  driven turbulence and starburst energy and mass injection are built in. Examples of what it
  does: shock tubes, blast waves and Kelvin-Helmholtz, Rayleigh-Taylor and Richtmyer-Meshkov
  instabilities; Jeans collapse and collapsing self-gravitating clouds; the cosmic web of gas and
  dark matter from cosmological initial conditions; driven supersonic turbulence; galactic winds
  blown out of a starburst disk; and inverse problems, such as optimising initial conditions with
  gradients taken through the solver. Non-equilibrium chemistry and cooling networks, radiation
  transport, sink particles and star formation with stellar dynamics and feedback through
  Torch/AMUSE, and block-based adaptive mesh refinement are included as experimental modules.
  Validated capabilities are tested against exact solutions or other codes (AthenaK, Arepo,
  RAMSES), whose reference solutions are published with the code.
type: software
authors:
